  "query_preview": "..High pressure will continue to build across the \nregion, remaining over the Desert Southwest through the weekend and \ninto early next week. Temperatures will warm a little more each day \nwith highs rising to 10-20 degrees above normal. The warmth looks to \npeak around Monday or Tuesday when the ridge starts to shift to the \neast which will allow for some slightly cooler air to filter down \ninto the region. However, temperatures will still remain around 5-10 \ndegrees above normal through the end of the week. \n\nA weak shortwave will cross the region today, mainly clipping the \nFour Corners region. Moisture remains very limited so not really \nexpecting much in the way of precipitation. A few sprinkles may be \nobserved, mainly across northeastern Arizona through this evening. \nMost areas will remain too dry at the surface with only some virga \npresent. As the wave passes by, the gradient will tighten ever so \nslightly and we will see west to northwest winds around 10-20 mph \nwith some gusts to 25-30 mph through this evening. \n\nAs we head towards mid week, the ridge will shift eastward and \nanother weak wave will move along the outside of the ridge, staying \nmostly to our west before crossing the area on Wednesday. Winds will \nturn more to the south-southwest, allowing for decent moisture \nadvection starting on Monday. Most areas will remain dry through \nmidweek but some isolated showers are possible, especially near the \nWhite Mountains on Monday and Tuesday (and even a rumble or two of \nthunder may be heard). By Wednesday, as the wave crosses the region, \nwe may see some additional isolated showers or thunderstorms across \nthe higher terrain, but QPF amounts look to be very light. \n\nThursday and beyond...behind the wave it looks like we see a trough \nsettle across the western states with the ridge having slid further \nto the east. The cooler but still above normal temperatures will \nhang around through the end of the week with drier conditions \nreturning. Expect those breezy southwest winds to continue each \nafternoon.",
